<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Horizontal Stretches and Compressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01056</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Horizontal Stretches and Compressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>76e3ba95-00c6-4c95-9508-25f577bd8d6d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Horizontal Stretches and Compressions</title>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction End for Question 1-->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Access the <link url="https://openstax.org/r/desmos-guide">Desmos
        guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
      </note>
   

      <!-- NOTE FROM RYAN: THIS IMAGE DOES NOT SEEM TO APPEAR IN RAISE WEB<media alt="...">
         <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_11_01.png"/></media>
          -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">   <para id="para-00008">1. On the same coordinate grid, graph the following linear functions. You do not need to
          label the lines in the graphing tool.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Line Label</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>i</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>j</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>k</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
               <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
            </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your work:</para>
          <media alt="A graph compares three functions: f of x equals x is the red dashed line, f of 2 times x is the blue solid line, and f of one-half times x is the green dotted line, each plotted on a coordinate plane with labeled axes.  Each line goes through the orgin and has a positive slope.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_01.png" height="300"/>
          </media>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction End for Question 10-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">2. What is the difference between what you entered for the functions of
            lines <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>j</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>k</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> in comparison to the
            function for line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>j</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: Line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>i</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is the parent linear
            function. Lines <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>j</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>k</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> are the result of
            function f where the input is different from the original <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-values.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 11: Click to Reveal -->
   

      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00029">3. Now, graph lines <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>n</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> on the same graph as <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>i</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mi>k</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
      <!-- Table for Question 11 -->
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00002">
        <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Line Label</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your work:</para>
          <media alt="A graph shows lines for functions: f of x equals x is the orange dashed line, f of 2 times x is the blue solid line, f of one-half times x is the green solid line, m of x equals 2 times x is the red dotted line, and n of x equals one-half times x is the black dashed line. All lines go through the origin with a positive slope.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_02.png" height="300"/>
          </media>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!-- Click to Reveal Content for Question 11 -->
      <!-- Question 12 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">4. How does line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>j</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, representing <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, compare
            to line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, representing <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer: The same points are in both functions. The
            lines appear to be exactly the same.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!-- Question 13 -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00012">5. How does line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>k</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, representing <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, compare
            to line <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, representing <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: The same points are in both functions. The
            lines appear to be exactly the same.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <para id="para-00014"><term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">Horizontal dilations</term>
        that stretch and compress <emphasis>linear</emphasis> functions are difficult to identify
        from graphs because they can appear as if they are vertical dilations. However, they are
        different because:</para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item>The changes to the function are applied to the input values of a function when the
          transformation is a <emphasis>horizontal dilation</emphasis>.</item>
        <item>The changes to the function are applied to the output values of a function when the
          transformation is a <emphasis>vertical dilation</emphasis>.</item>
      </list>
      <!-- Question 14: CTA Reveal Interaction Start -->
      <para id="para-00080">6. Now graph the lines given below on the same coordinate grid.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00003">
        <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Line Label</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>i</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>j</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>p</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>q</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00015"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your work:</para>
          <media alt="Line graph comparing four functions: f of x equals x is the dashed orange line, f of 2 times x is the solid blue line, f of 3 times x is the dotted green line, and f of 4 times x is the solid red line, each with increasing steepness. All lines go through the origin with a positive slope.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_03.png" height="300"/>
          </media>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!-- Question 14: CTA Reveal Interaction End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start for Question 15-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00017">7. As the coefficient on the input increases, what is happening to the
            graph?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00018">Compare your answer: The line gets closer and closer to the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-axis because the function
            is being horizontally compressed.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction End for Question 15-->
      <!-- Question 16: CTA Reveal Interaction Start -->
      <para id="para-00081">8. Graph the lines given below on the same coordinate grid.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00004">
        <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Line Label</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>i</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>k</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>s</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00019"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your work:</para>
          <media alt="A graph shows four lines representing functions: f of x equals x is the dashed orange line, f of one-half times x is the solid blue line, f of one-fourth times x is the green dotted line, and f of one-tenth times  x is the solid red line.  All lines go through the origin with a positive slope.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_04.png" height="300"/>
          </media>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!-- Question 16: CTA Reveal Interaction End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start for Question 17-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00021">9. As the coefficient on the input approaches 0, what is happening to
            the graph?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00022">Compare your answer: The line gets farther from the <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>-axis because the function
            is being horizontally stretched.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction End for Question 17-->
      <!-- Question 18: CTA Reveal Interaction Start -->
      <para id="para-00082">10. Graph the lines given below on the same coordinate grid.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00005">
        <tgroup cols="2">
<tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Line Label</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Function</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Line <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>u</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
              <entry><m:math>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mfrac>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:mfrac>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <media alt="...">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00010">
        <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00023"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00010">
          <para id="para-00024">Compare your work:</para>
          <media alt="A graph shows two lines: a solid blue line for f of one-half times x, increasing from bottom left to top right, and a dotted green line for f of one-half times x, decreasing from top left to bottom right.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_05.png" height="300"/>
          </media>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!-- Question 18: CTA Reveal Interaction End -->
      <para id="para-00025">In the function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> is acting as the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">horizontal stretch</term> or <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">horizontal compression</term> of the parent
        function. It dilates the graph.</para>
      <list id="list-00007">
        <item>When <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is negative, there is also a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00004">horizontal reflection</term> of the graph over
          the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-axis.</item>
        <item>Multiplying the function's input by <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> horizontally compresses the
          graph of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> by a factor of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> units if <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&gt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</item>
        <item>The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> horizontally expands the graph
          of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> by a factor of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> units if <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</item>
      </list>

      <media alt="Graph with eight colorful lines showing transformations of f of x equals a times x. Each line, labeled in matching colors, represents different functions: f of one-tenth times x, f of one-fourth times x, f of one-half times x, f of 2 times x, f of 3 times x, f of negative x, f of negative one-half times x.">
        <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_12_06.png" height="300"/>
      </media>

      <note id="note-00002">
        <title>Horizontal Dilations of a Function</title>
        <para id="para-00026">A horizontal stretch
        or compression “transforms” the parent function into another function by horizontally
        dilating the graph by “b” units.</para>
        <para id="para-00027"> Horizontal Dilation → <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00028">If the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> value is negative, the graph is
        reflected over the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>y</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-axis. If the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> value is greater than 1, the graph is
        horizontally compressed. If the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>b</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> value is between 0 and 1, the graph
        is horizontally stretched.</para>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00003">
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
        <para id="para-00003"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_4_11_5_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Horizontal Transformations of Linear Functions</title>
        <para id="para-00030">Horizontal transformations are the result of altering the input values
          before applying the function rule. Vertical transformations result from altering the
          output values after the function rule has been applied.</para>
          <para id="para-00077"><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00031">Describe how <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is
            transformed into <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00032">
            <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
          </para>
          <para id="para-00033">First, graph the given function, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, the
            parent linear function.</para>

          <media alt="A thick, red dashed line with a positive slope passes through the origin on an x-y grid, extending from the lower left to the upper right. Both axes are labeled and ticked from negative 4 to 5 for x and negative 4 to 7 for y.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_01.png" height="400"/>
          </media>

          <para id="para-00034">Now, let’s examine an input value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> using <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <list id="list-00008">
            <item>For the parent function <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>,
              all points on the line are represented by <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
            <item>So, when <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>, then <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              becomes <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
            <item>That means this point is located at <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
          </list>
          <para id="para-00035">In order to get the same output value for <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> as we
            had for <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, we need
            to figure out how to change the input value. </para>
          <list id="list-00009">
            <item>Because our transformed equation is subtracting three from what we input, we have
              to increase the prior input value by 3 units to yield the same output as <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
            <item>The input value that is 3 units larger than <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> is 5. In the
              transformed equation, we must use <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</item>
          </list>
          <para id="para-00036">So, in the original equation where <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> resulted in <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, in the transformed
            equation, we have to use <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> in order for <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>

          <media alt="A graph shows two lines: a red dashed line with open circles at (2,2), and a blue solid line passing through the points (2, negative 1) and (5,2) with an open circle.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_02.png" height="400"/>
          </media>

          <para id="para-00037">On the graph, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is located 3 units to the
            right of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. So, the transformed
            equation of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is 3
            units to the right of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00038">Horizontal transformations are represented by the rule <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> where <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> indicates the number of
            units to the right or left that the graph is shifted.</para>

          <para id="para-00078"><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00039">Graph <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> when <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Then, determine the
            equation of the transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00040">
            <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
          </para>
          <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Graph the given function, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00042"> The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is (0, -3)
            and the slope is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00076">For horizontal shifts, it is helpful to identify the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept.
            On our graph, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept or zero is located
            at (6,0). </para>
          <para id="para-00043">
            <media alt="A graph with a dashed red line with a positive slope. There is a white open circle on the line at the point (6, 0).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_03.png" height="400"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Apply the designated transformation.
            Remember that the rule for a horizontal shift is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. If we
            rewrite <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> in an
            equivalent form, we can make it is easier to see <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00045"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> That
            means <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00046">So, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            represents a horizontal shift of two units to the left. On our graph, we move the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept two units left from (6, 0) to (4, 0).</para>
          <para id="para-00047">
            <media alt="A graph with a solid blue line and a dashed red line are parallel on a grid. The blue line passes through points (4, 0), and (6,0), which are marked with open circles. Axes range from -2 to 8 on x and -5 to 6 on y.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_04.png" height="400"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00048">The orange dashed line is the graph of the original function and the
            blue solid line is the transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Determine the equation of the
            transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00050">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the
            transformed line is (0, -2) and the slope of the line is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            or <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            .</para>
          <para id="para-00051">That means the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00052">Want to check your work? We can check our equation algebraically by
            substituting <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> into <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00053"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00054"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>

          <para id="para-00079"><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
          <para id="para-00055">Graph <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> when <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Then, determine the
            equation of the transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00056">
            <emphasis>Solution</emphasis>
          </para>
          <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Graph the given function, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00058">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is (0, -3) and
            the slope is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            .</para>
          <para id="para-00059">For horizontal shifts, it is helpful to identify the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept. On our graph, the <emphasis effect="italics">
            x</emphasis>-intercept or zero is located at (6,0).</para>
          <para id="para-00060">
            <media alt="A graph with a dashed red line with a positive slope. There is a white open circle on the line at the point (6, 0).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_05.png" height="400"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Apply the designated transformation.
            Remember that the rule for a horizontal shift is <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. And,
            when the value of <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> is greater than 1, the
            function experiences a horizontal compression.</para>
          <para id="para-00062">As a result of the horizontal compression, the linear function
            should look steeper because it is being compressed by a scale factor of 4.</para>
          <para id="para-00063">On our graph, this means the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept
            is compressed from (6, 0) to (1.5, 0).</para>
          <para id="para-00064">
            <media alt="A graph showing a blue line with positive slope crossing the y-axis at -3, and a red dashed line with positive slope crossing the y-axis at negative 3. The lines intersect at point (0, negative 3) with an open white circle. There are also open white circles at (1.5, 0) and (6, 0).">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_14_14_06.png" height="400"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00065">The orange dashed line is the graph of the original function and the
            blue solid line is the transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00066"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Determine the equation of the
            transformed function.</para>
          <para id="para-00067">The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept of the
            transformed line is (0, -3) and the slope of the line is 3/1.5 or 2.</para>
          <para id="para-00068">That means the equation <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00069">Want to check your work? We can check our equation algebraically by
            substituting 4x into <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
          <para id="para-00070"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00071"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>✔</para>

        <note class="try" id="note-00004">
          <title>Horizontal Transformations of Linear Functions</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
            <problem id="prob-00011">
              <para id="para-00072">Name the transformation to go from the parent function, <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>, to <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
              <para id="para-00073"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00011">
              <para id="para-00074">Here is how to determine the transformation:</para>
              <para id="para-00075">Because <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          defines a horizontal shift, the transformation <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          moves the parent function 1 unit to the right.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>